# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2A - A faithful port of EVOLVE_NASH, with a Strang variant
# ===========================================================================
#
# Porting the one piece of original numerical work in the legacy C++ - a Bessel-expanded split-operator propagator - and wiring it into the language.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# The legacy SolveIt C++ contained one genuinely original numerical method,
# about twenty lines long, called EVOLVE_NASH. Everything else in its
# quantum tree was either standard Crank-Nicolson or a licence-encumbered
# library routine. This stage ported it.
#
# THE SCHEME, IN FULL
#
# Discretise H = -hbar^2/2m d2/dx2 + V(x) on a PERIODIC grid of spacing h.
# Write S for the one-site shift, (S psi)_j = psi_(j+1). Then
#
#     H = kappa (2 - S - S^-1) + V,        kappa = hbar^2 / (2 m h^2)
#
# and the time step splits into a diagonal part and an off-diagonal part:
#
#     exp(-i H dt/hbar) ~ exp(-i L (1+v)) * exp(i (L/2)(S + S^-1))
#
#     L = hbar dt / (m h^2)          v_j = V_j m h^2 / hbar^2
#
# The first factor is diagonal - a pointwise phase. The second is the idea.
# S has Fourier symbol exp(i theta), so (S + S^-1)/2 has symbol cos theta,
# and the Jacobi-Anger expansion
#
#     exp(i L cos theta) = sum_M i^M J_M(L) exp(i M theta)
#
# turns the kinetic exponential into a FINITE-DIFFERENCE STENCIL whose
# coefficients are Bessel functions:
#
#     psi_j <- exp(-i L (1+v_j)) [ J_0(L) psi_j
#              + sum_(M=1..K) i^M J_M(L) (psi_(j-M) + psi_(j+M)) ]
#
# It is EXPLICIT and MATRIX-FREE - Crank-Nicolson solves a tridiagonal
# system every step; this solves nothing - and it is unitary at any step
# size, because each factor is unitary.
#
# WHY THE PORT IS 'FAITHFUL' AND WHAT THAT COST
#
# Every other port in this project runs clean-room: read the mathematics,
# never the encumbered code. That rule is exactly WRONG here, because the
# scheme is the author's own. So the port was checked against the ORIGINAL
# ALGORITHM: a statement-for-statement transliteration of the C++ loop lives
# in the test module, and the port must match it at the original's own
# constants (Lambda = 0.92, NumOrder = 16). Measured agreement: 3.5e-16
# after one step, 1.9e-13 after a thousand - floating-point summation order
# and nothing else.
#
# The one encumbered dependency inside that kernel - 'bessj', a Numerical
# Recipes routine - was NOT transliterated; the clean-room Bessel routine
# supplies the same J_M(Lambda).
#
# THE SPLITTING, AND THE SECOND-ORDER VARIANT
#
# The original orders the factors as Lie-Trotter, which is FIRST order in
# dt. Putting half a potential phase on each side of the stencil (Strang)
# is SECOND order for essentially no extra cost, because consecutive steps
# fuse their adjacent half-phases. Measured error ratios per halving of dt:
# 2.00 for Lie, 4.00 for Strang, exactly as theory says.
#
# Lie remains the DEFAULT, because the default behaviour of a port has to
# be what the original does.
#
# BOUNDARY CONDITIONS - READ THIS BEFORE INTERPRETING ANY RESULT
#
# The Nash method is PERIODIC: the two ends of the domain are identified and
# a packet leaving the right edge re-enters at the left. Crank-Nicolson in
# this crate is DIRICHLET: the walls reflect. They are different physics on
# the same grid. The status line always says which is in force.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It never refers you to another
# notebook, another README or another document: everything needed to build,
# run, display and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# Lines beginning with '#' are comments and are ignored by the interpreter.
# Every other line is a command, executed in the order written.
#
# STEP 1 - GET THE CODE AND A RUST TOOLCHAIN.
#
#     git clone https://github.com/once-ere/rustSimulate.git
#     cd rustSimulate
#
#   There are no submodules and no external crates to fetch: every
#   dependency is vendored in the tree. If 'cargo' is not found, install
#   Rust from https://rustup.rs and re-open the terminal.
#
# STEP 2 - BUILD ONCE. From the repository root (the directory holding
# Cargo.toml):
#
#     cargo build -p posim --release
#
#   The first build compiles the workspace and takes a few minutes. Every
#   later run is immediate. This produces ./target/release/posim .
#
# STEP 3 - RUN THIS NOTEBOOK. Any ONE of these three forms works:
#
#     ./target/release/posim --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2A.posim
#     ./target/release/posim --script   StageNbks/Stage_2A.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2A.posim
#
#   --notebook  executes the file, prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript,
#               and then LEAVES YOU AT THE PROMPT with all state intact.
#               This is the mode to use: it is what makes the session
#               interactive, and it is required for the live window.
#   --script    executes the file and EXITS. Use it for a plain
#               reproducible transcript; you cannot interact afterwards.
#   cargo run   identical to the first form, but re-checks the build
#               first. Use it if you are editing the source.
#
# STEP 4 - WORK BY HAND (optional). Start the bare interpreter:
#
#     ./target/release/posim
#
#   and paste the command lines from this file one at a time. Type HELP
#   for the complete command language, and %quit to leave. Other useful
#   magics: %history, %rerun <n>, %edit <n> <text>, %save <file>, %reset.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNNING THIS STAGE AS AN INTERACTIVE SIMULATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THIS STAGE IS A QUANTUM PROBLEM: a wavefunction on a grid, not a set of
# rigid bodies. Be clear about what that means for the display, because
# the wrong expectation here wastes real time:
#
#   The 3-D scene window draws RIGID BODIES ONLY. This notebook creates no
#   rigid bodies at all, so 'scene create' here opens a window that
#   correctly reports 'showing 0 entities'. That is not a failure and not
#   a bug - there is genuinely no rigid body in this stage to draw.
#
# THE INTERACTIVE VIEWER FOR A WAVEFUNCTION IS THE HTML FILM written by
# 'qm animate', and it is interactive in the real sense: it has playback
# controls and a frame scrubber, and it recomputes nothing when you use
# them, so scrubbing is instant.
#
# METHOD 1 - ONE COMMAND, THEN OPEN THE FILM.
#
#     cargo build -p posim --release
#     ./target/release/posim --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2A.posim
#
#   This writes two films into the StageNbks folder:
#
#       StageNbks/out_2A_nash_periodic.html
#       StageNbks/out_2A_cayley_reflecting.html
#
#   'qm animate' prints the path and the frame count as it writes each
#   one. The files are regenerated every run, so they are deliberately
#   not tracked by git.
#
# METHOD 2 - OPEN THE FILM. It is a self-contained HTML file: no server,
# no network, no install. Use whichever line matches your system:
#
#     Linux:    xdg-open StageNbks/out_2A_nash_periodic.html
#     macOS:    open StageNbks/out_2A_nash_periodic.html
#     Windows:  start StageNbks\out_2A_nash_periodic.html
#
#   Or open your browser and use File > Open, or drag the file onto a
#   browser window. Any modern browser works; nothing else is required.
#
# THE FILM'S CONTROLS, exactly:
#
#   It STARTS PLAYING BY ITSELF and LOOPS forever, about 22 frames per
#   second regardless of how many frames you asked for.
#
#   [Pause]     toggles playback. The label changes to 'Play' when paused
#               and back to 'Pause' when running - the label always names
#               what the NEXT click will do.
#   [Restart]   jumps to frame 0. It does NOT pause: if it was playing it
#               keeps playing, from the beginning.
#   slider      drag to any frame. Dragging PAUSES playback automatically
#               (the button flips to 'Play'), so you can step through a
#               reflection or a tunnelling event frame by frame.
#
#   The readout to the right of the slider shows, for the frame on screen:
#
#       t = <time>   frame <i>/<n>   norm = <total>   transmitted = <part>
#
#   'norm' is the conserved quantity: watch it while you scrub. 'transmitted'
#   is the probability in the region marked in green.
#
#   The colour key under the canvas: blue is |psi|^2, orange is V(x) drawn
#   to a scale that fits the axes, green marks the transmitted region.
#
# METHOD 3 - DRIVE IT YOURSELF, INTERACTIVELY. Start the bare interpreter:
#
#     cargo build -p posim --release
#     ./target/release/posim
#
#   Type the setup lines from the 'RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO' section
#   below, then write your own film with any end time and frame count:
#
#       qm animate "my_run.html" 2.0 frames 300
#
#   More frames means a smoother film and a bigger file; the end time is
#   in the same units as the rest of the stage. Between films you can
#   change the method, the potential or the boundary and animate again -
#   'qm' on its own prints the current configuration, so you can always
#   see what you are about to propagate.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPENING THE WINDOW IN A BROWSER - EXACTLY WHAT HAPPENS, ON EVERY OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 'scene create' prints THREE lines, for example:
#
#     scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#     (asked your desktop to open it; if no window appeared, open that address yourself)
#     showing 12 entities; SCENE START begins the evolution — HELP lists all scene commands
#
# The MIDDLE line tells you what was actually attempted, and it has two
# forms. Read it: it is the difference between waiting for a window that
# is coming and waiting for one that is not.
#
#     (asked your desktop to open it; ...)     a browser launch was made
#     (no browser was launched — open that address yourself)
#
# The port is chosen by the operating system afresh on every run. To pin
# it, give the port:  'scene create 8080'  always serves
# http://127.0.0.1:8080/ . If that port is busy, 'scene create' reports
# the bind error; pick another.
#
# posim makes ONE best-effort attempt to open your browser, by running
# exactly:
#
#     xdg-open <url>
#
# xdg-open is a Linux/BSD utility. That has consequences worth stating
# plainly, because a window that does not appear is otherwise alarming:
#
#   * Linux/BSD with xdg-utils installed - the browser opens by itself,
#     and the middle line reads 'asked your desktop to open it'.
#   * macOS - NOTHING OPENS, and nothing is broken: xdg-open does not
#     exist there, so the middle line reads 'no browser was launched'.
#     Copy the printed URL into any browser, or in a second terminal:
#         open http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#   * Windows - NOTHING OPENS, same reason, same middle line. Copy the
#     printed URL into any browser, or in a second terminal:
#         start http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#   * Headless server or SSH session - nothing opens. Forward the port and
#     browse from your own machine:
#         ssh -L 41234:127.0.0.1:41234 <user>@<host>
#     then open http://127.0.0.1:41234/ locally.
#
# Setting the environment variable POSIM_NO_BROWSER to any value suppresses
# the attempt entirely. The URL is still printed and still works; this is
# what the automated tests use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 ./target/release/posim
#
# The page is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript served by posim itself over a
# websocket. Nothing is installed, nothing is fetched from the network, and
# the listener is bound to 127.0.0.1, so it is not reachable from another
# machine by design.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SCENE WINDOW: EVERY CONTROL, AND ITS TYPED EQUIVALENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TOOLBAR, in the order it appears left to right:
#
#   [>]  Start      scene start        begin / resume forward evolution
#   [||] Pause      scene pause        freeze, keep the window live
#   [#]  Stop       scene stop         stop evolution AND clear history
#   [<]  Reverse    scene reverse      play backward through the history
#   [o]  Reset      scene reset        every value and the time return to
#                                      their initial values; Start then
#                                      runs the simulation again
#   [|<]            (button only)      one step backward
#   [>|]            (button only)      one step forward
#   dt [___] Set    scene set_time_step <dt>
#                                      simulated seconds per frame; type a
#                                      number in the box and press Set
#   [magnifier +]   scene zoom in      zoom in
#   [magnifier -]   scene zoom out     zoom out
#   [^]  View       (button only)      reset the camera to its home pose
#   Grid            (button only)      toggle the ground grid
#   Trails          (button only)      toggle motion trails
#   Labels          (button only)      toggle object labels
#   Contacts        (button only)      toggle contact-normal arrows drawn
#                                      at collision points
#   ?               (button only)      toggle the controls cheat-sheet
#
# MOUSE AND KEYBOARD, with the pointer over the window:
#
#   left-drag                rotate (orbit) around the scene
#   shift-drag or right-drag translate (pan) with the mouse
#   mouse wheel              zoom in / out
#   +  or  =                 zoom in
#   -  or  _                 zoom out
#   Left / Right arrow       translate the view left / right
#   Up / Down arrow          translate the view up / down
#   Space                    start / pause playback
#   R                        reset the view
#   G                        toggle the ground grid
#   T                        toggle motion trails
#   L                        toggle object labels
#   C                        toggle contact-normal arrows
#   H  or  ?                 toggle the controls cheat-sheet
#
#   READ THIS ONE TWICE, because the natural assumption is wrong: the
#   ARROW KEYS MOVE THE CAMERA, not the simulation. To advance the
#   physics one frame at a time use the [|<] and [>|] toolbar buttons.
#
# THE STATUS BAR along the bottom reports, live: body count, contact count,
# hidden count, simulated time, dt, energy, frames per second, camera yaw /
# pitch / distance, playback mode, recorded-history depth, and the websocket
# connection state.
#
# TYPED COMMANDS, available at the In[n]:= prompt while the window is open.
# The window and the prompt drive the SAME state, so a typed command and
# the button that mirrors it cannot disagree:
#
#   scene status                 connection / camera / playback report
#   scene refresh                re-sync the window after adding, removing
#                                or editing bodies
#   scene redraw                 force a full redraw of every window
#   scene hide <n> | all         hide entities
#   scene show <n> | all         show them again
#   scene translate dx dy [dz]   move the view
#   scene rotate dyaw dpitch     rotate the view
#   scene zoom in | out | <f>    zoom (f > 1 zooms in)
#   scene set_time_step <dt>     simulated seconds per frame
#   scene start | stop | pause | reverse | reset
#   scene events                 read asynchronous events from the window
#   scene close                  shut the window and free the port
#                                (alias: scene destroy)
#   energy                       print the total energy, live, while it runs
#   HELP                         the complete command language
#
# ONE BEHAVIOUR THAT LOOKS LIKE A FAULT AND IS NOT. 'scene reverse'
# immediately after opening answers:
#
#     nothing to reverse - no forward history recorded yet
#
# Reverse replays RECORDED history. Until the scene has actually run
# forward there is nothing recorded to play back. Let it run a few seconds,
# then Reverse works. 'scene stop' clears that history deliberately, so
# Reverse is empty again after a Stop.
#
# TROUBLESHOOTING, precisely:
#
#   'no scene window - run SCENE CREATE first'
#       The window was closed, or never opened. Type 'scene create'.
#   The window opens but says 'showing 0 entities'
#       The world genuinely holds no bodies. Build them first, then
#       'scene refresh'. An empty window is an honest report, not a bug.
#   The port is already in use
#       'scene close', then 'scene create <another port>'.
#   The page loads but never moves
#       Playback is not running: press Start, or type 'scene start'.
#   Bodies jump through each other
#       The time step is too large for their speed. Lower it with
#       'scene set_time_step <smaller dt>' or the dt box and Set.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A free particle on a periodic domain, propagated by the ported scheme.

# The domain and its resolution.
qm grid 0 20 400

# A free particle: no potential at all.
qm potential zero

# A Gaussian wavepacket at x0 = 17, width sigma = 0.8, mean wavenumber 6.
# It is launched near the RIGHT-hand edge on purpose, so that the periodic
# boundary is exercised rather than merely asserted.
qm packet 17 0.8 6

# Select the ported propagator. 'nash' alone is the original Lie ordering;
# 'nash strang' is the second-order variant. Both are periodic.
qm method nash strang

# Report the whole configuration, including which boundary is in force.
qm

# Where is the packet now? Almost all of it is in the right-hand fifth.
qm prob 0 5
qm prob 15 20

# Propagate. 400 steps to t = 0.85.
qm run 0.85 steps 400

# And now? Under the PERIODIC Nash method the packet has crossed the seam
# and arrived at the far left: expect roughly 0.98 in [0,5] and 0.02 in
# [15,20]. Under Crank-Nicolson the same run reflects off the wall and
# leaves ~0.999 in [15,20]. That contrast IS the boundary condition.
qm prob 0 5
qm prob 15 20

# The norm is conserved to rounding regardless of step size, because the
# scheme is a product of unitary factors. This is a property of the METHOD,
# not a consequence of choosing a small dt.
qm norm

# The energy of a free packet is a constant of the motion here.
qm energy

# For contrast, repeat with the reflecting method. Same grid, same packet.
qm reset
qm grid 0 20 400
qm potential zero
qm packet 17 0.8 6
qm method cayley
qm run 0.85 steps 400
qm prob 0 5
qm prob 15 20


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOW WRITE THE FILM. This is the display for this notebook. It rebuilds the
# periodic setup and writes a self-contained HTML animation of |psi|^2, then
# a second one under reflecting walls so the two can be compared side by
# side. posim prints each path as it is written; open them as described in
# the GUI section above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
qm reset
qm grid 0 20 400
qm potential zero
qm packet 17 0.8 6
qm method nash strang
qm animate "StageNbks/out_2A_nash_periodic.html" 1.5 frames 200

qm reset
qm grid 0 20 400
qm potential zero
qm packet 17 0.8 6
qm method cayley
qm animate "StageNbks/out_2A_cayley_reflecting.html" 1.5 frames 200

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2A NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
